# Use Entities (Not Hardcoded Enums) for Dynamic UI Lists

**Rule:** When rendering a list of options that actually depends on the API or can change over time (routes, seats, services, etc.), do not model it as a hardcoded `enum`. Hardcoding forces an app release whenever the server adds an option. Instead create an Entity model in `domain/`, store a `List<Entity>` in the Cubit/Bloc state (loaded from API or mock), and render from that list. Reserve enums for genuinely fixed, client-known sets.

### ❌ Bad Example
```dart
enum MyLine { lineA, lineB }

extension MyLineX on MyLine {
  String get title => this == MyLine.lineA ? 'Line A' : 'Line B';
}

Row(children: MyLine.values.map((e) => Text(e.title)).toList());
```

### ✅ Good Example
```dart
class LineEntity extends Equatable {
  final String id;
  final String title;
  const LineEntity({required this.id, required this.title});
  @override
  List<Object?> get props => [id, title];
}

// state holds List<LineEntity> availableLines, rendered via BlocBuilder
Row(children: state.availableLines.map((e) => Text(e.title)).toList());
```
